    ":question/id": "d6501a20-6ebd-5786-870e-3527423b6332",
    ":question/math-academy-id": "e-449",
    ":question/problem": "Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int \\dfrac{3}{5x+2}\\textrm{d}x}.$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "First, let $u=5x+2.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 5\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = \\dfrac 1 5 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{5x+2}\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{5x+2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|5x+2|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"
  }
